Who Needs an Ocular Prosthesis?

An ocular prosthesis is fitted after the surgical removal of an eye (enucleation or evisceration) or in cases of phthisis bulbi (a shrunken, non-functional eye). Common reasons for eye removal include:

Severe trauma with an unsalvageable globe
Painful blind eye from end-stage glaucoma or retinal detachment
Intraocular tumours (retinoblastoma in children, uveal melanoma in adults)
Severe infection (endophthalmitis) with an unsalvageable eye

Orbital implants (made of hydroxyapatite or porous polyethylene) are placed at the time of surgery to maintain orbital volume and provide motility for the prosthesis.

The Prosthesis Fitting Process

Step 1 – Post-Surgical Healing (4–6 weeks) After enucleation or evisceration, a conformer (clear plastic placeholder) is placed in the socket. This maintains the conjunctival fornices (the spaces the prosthesis will occupy) during healing.

Step 2 – Prosthesis Fitting Once healed, an individually crafted acrylic prosthesis is fitted. The ocularist (prosthetics specialist) takes an impression of the socket for a custom fit, then hand-paints the iris, pupil, and scleral vessels to match the fellow eye as closely as possible.

Step 3 – Review and Adjustment The prosthesis is reviewed at 1 month, then annually. The acrylic surface requires polishing every 6–12 months to maintain comfortable wear and optimal appearance.

Step 4 – Long-Term Care Patients are taught how to insert, remove, and clean the prosthesis. Good socket hygiene prevents socket discharge and conjunctival complications.

Children and Ocular Prosthetics

Children with retinoblastoma or congenital eye absence require prosthetics management from an early age. In growing children, the socket and prosthesis must be regularly updated as the face develops. Early fitting is important for normal orbital and facial growth on the affected side.

